.TH NETWTOP 1 "July 2026" "netwtop 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netwtop \- interactive per-user network traffic monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netwtop
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B netwtop
is a terminal network monitor inspired by nvitop. It displays authoritative
upload and download rates for a selected interface together with best-effort
per-user and per-command network attribution.
.PP
The interface panel and application panel are separate accounting domains.
On Linux, interface totals come from kernel interface counters, while user and
command attribution comes from cumulative TCP counters exposed by
.BR ss (8)
across all interfaces in the current network namespace.
.PP
The default sampling interval is 0.5 seconds. Interactive table output updates
the existing terminal frame in place.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " " SECONDS
Set the sampling interval in 0.1-second steps. The minimum is 0.1 seconds and
the default is 0.5 seconds.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-device " " INTERFACE
Start with the specified network interface. The default is the interface used
by the default route.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-count " " NUMBER
Stop after NUMBER reports. By default, run until interrupted.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mode " " MODE
Select the display mode. MODE is
.BR auto ,
.BR compact ,
or
.BR full .
.TP
.BR \-\-two-column-width " " COLUMNS
Use the side-by-side upload and download layout at or above this terminal
width. The default is 100 columns.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " FORMAT
Select
.BR table ,
.BR csv ,
or
.B jsonl
output. The default is
.BR table .
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " FILE
Write reports to FILE instead of standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-append
Append to the output file instead of replacing it. This option requires
.BR \-\-output .
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the installed netwtop version, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show command-line options, interactive keys, backend information, and
privilege guidance, then exit.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
.TP
.B q, Q
Quit and restore the terminal.
.TP
.B r, R
Refresh immediately.
.TP
.B +, -
Decrease or increase the refresh interval.
.TP
.B a, c, f
Select auto, compact, or full display mode.
.TP
.B Left, Right
Switch the selected physical, virtual, bridge, or loopback interface.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move the highlighted user, or move within the highlighted command list.
.TP
.B j, k
Scroll users, or commands in checked-user mode.
.TP
.B PageUp, PageDown
Scroll by the visible user or command page.
.TP
.B [, ]
Scroll the selected user's fixed command viewport.
.TP
.B Space, x
Check or uncheck the selected user.
.TP
.B Mouse click, wheel
Select rows and scroll the user or command area when terminal mouse reporting
is available.
.SH PRIVILEGES
.B netwtop
does not invoke
.BR sudo (8)
or elevate its own privileges.
.PP
As a normal user, command details are shown only for the effective UID. Other
regular users may retain aggregate upload and download rows, but their command
details are hidden.
.PP
When run as root, command details are shown for all visible regular users. If a
Linux PID is available, the effective process UID determines ownership, so a
network command executed through sudo is attributed to root.
.SH DATA SOURCES
.TP
.B Linux
Interface counters are read from sysfs with
.I /proc/net/dev
as a fallback. TCP attribution uses
.BR ss (8)
from iproute2, and process metadata uses
.BR ps (1).
.TP
.B macOS
Interface counters use the system
.BR netstat (1),
.BR route (8),
and
.BR ifconfig (8)
commands. Per-process network counters use the built-in
.BR nettop (1)
command.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/netwtop/
Installed shell modules and the AWK table renderer.
.TP
.I /etc/login.defs
On Linux, UID_MIN is used as the regular-user threshold when readable.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the interactive monitor using the default interface:
.PP
.nf
netwtop
.fi
.PP
Monitor interface eno1 with a one-second sampling interval:
.PP
.nf
netwtop --device eno1 --interval 1
.fi
.PP
Emit five JSON Lines reports:
.PP
.nf
netwtop --format jsonl --interval 0.5 --count 5
.fi
.PP
Enable cross-user command details on Linux:
.PP
.nf
sudo netwtop
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
Linux application attribution is TCP-focused and is not wire-equivalent to
interface RX and TX counters. Short-lived connections between samples can be
missed. Shared sockets cannot always be divided exactly among processes, and
only the current network namespace is visible.
